  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at half height. It is steeper along x, so overshoot shows there first. For any batch size, swings along x grow once learning rate > 2/3; with batch 64 there is no noise and that is exactly when the run diverges.",
    "Each step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise has spread 2·√((64−b)/(63b)) per direction for batch b: largest at 1, exactly zero at 64 (all points).",
    "The orange arrow is the next mini-batch step and the dark arrow is the full-data step (−learning rate × true slope). Both are drawn at 2× their true length.",
    "Diverged: the loss goes above 8, the highest loss on the drawn bowl. Positions off the bowl are drawn at its edge. The Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The ball takes 5 steps a second, up to 40 per run. A step lowers the loss only on average; with small batches a single step can raise it.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed sequence: Reset replays it from the start, and each new run uses the next values. A new run keeps the last path as a ghost.",
    "Changing a setting mid-run changes only the steps that follow."
  ]
}